= Event overview

The Treasury cash-futures basis trade is not an event with a single date. It is a trade that grew, quietly, into a systemic position and then, twice, showed what happens when it unwinds.

The trade is simple: buy a cash Treasury note, sell the matching Treasury futures contract, finance the cash bond in the repo market, and pocket the small gap between the cash price and the futures price (scaled by the conversion factor), the _basis_. The basis converges to zero at futures delivery, so the profit is, in the textbook telling, the difference between the repo rate you pay and the implied repo rate the package earns. The gap is tiny, a few basis points, so the trade only pays if it is levered enormously. That is the whole story, and the whole problem.

By September 2025 the trade had, on the Federal Reserve's own decomposition of SEC Form PF data, reached approximately *\$830 billion*, about double its early-2020 peak, 35% of hedge funds' \$2.4 trillion long Treasury exposure, and 3.5% of privately held Treasuries. Hedge funds' gross Treasury exposure had doubled since 2023 to *\$4.0 trillion* (\$2.4tn long / \$1.6tn short), financed by *\$3.0 trillion* of repo borrowing, and the 50 largest funds held about 90% of it (Monin, Federal Reserve FEDS Notes, 22 June 2026). A CFTC-based proxy put the trade above \$1tn in notional value in May 2025 (Patel, Chicago Fed Letter 516). A different method and date from Monin's Form PF decomposition, so the two figures are not directly comparable. Federal Reserve officials publicly flagged these positions in late 2025 as a systemic vulnerability, echoing March 2020. The trade's two stress tests frame this report: March 2020, when the "dash for cash" broke Treasury liquidity: hedge funds net-sold approximately \$173bn of Treasuries, concentrated in funds identified as likely basis traders (Banegas, Monin & Petrasek 2021), and large basis traders alone sold an estimated \$91–105bn while their short futures fell from \$659bn to \$554bn (Barth & Kahn, OFR Working Paper 21-01). The Fed announced it would buy at least \$500bn of Treasuries (15 March 2020) and in fact bought about \$1.6tn from March to May 2020 (Kashyap, Stein, Wallen & Younger 2025). The unwind amplified a shock driven by the flight to cash; it was not the shock's primary cause, and large dealers provided more repo funding during the crisis, not less (Kruttli et al. 2025); and April 2025, when tariff announcements hit Treasury yields and liquidity. Though, importantly, the documented April 2025 unwind was in the _related_ swap-spread trade (about \$60bn unwound in April, a further \$40bn in May), while the cash-futures basis trade kept growing to \$830bn by September. Keeping those two trades distinct matters, and this report does so throughout.

A dated positioning observation anchors the scale in the present: as of Tuesday 3 November 2026 (CFTC Commitments of Traders, released 6 November), leveraged funds were net short 2,262,310 contracts in 10-year Treasury futures (−38.0% of open interest), against asset managers net long 3,030,236 contracts. That net short is, overwhelmingly, the futures side of the basis trade, not a directional bet on yields.

The unwind mechanism is what makes a small, apparently hedged trade systemic. A basis fund's balance sheet is almost entirely borrowed money: a 2% haircut means a 2% fall in the bond's value wipes out its equity, and a margin call on the futures leg must be met in cash the same day. When many funds hold the same trade, the same shock hits them all at once, and the only way to raise cash is to sell the cash bond, which pushes its price down, widens the basis further, and triggers the next round of margin calls and haircut increases. The trade's risk is therefore not in any single fund's position but in the correlation of their funding: they all need cash on the same day, from the same lenders, against the same collateral.

= The fixed income securities involved

*Cash Treasuries (the long leg).* A Treasury note is a plain fixed-coupon bond: semi-annual coupons, principal at maturity, backed by the US government. The trade usually uses the cheapest-to-deliver (CTD) bond into the futures contract, often an off-the-run note, because the futures price is set by the cheapest bond to deliver. What the trade buys is therefore not "the 10-year" in the abstract but a specific CUSIP with a specific coupon, maturity and price. On 11 November 2026 the CTD into the December 2026 10-year futures contract was the *3 7/8% Treasury note due 15 August 2033, CUSIP 91282CHT1* (CME Treasury Analytics): 3.875% coupon, 6.7 years to maturity, conversion factor 0.8870, yield 4.894% at the observed Nov 10 close (CME reported 4.9023%).

*Treasury futures (the short leg).* A CME Treasury futures contract (the 10-year note future, TY, \$100,000 face) does not reference one bond. The short may deliver any bond from a defined basket; to make different coupons and maturities comparable, the exchange applies a _conversion factor_ (CF) to each, and the invoice amount on delivery is CF × futures price plus accrued interest. The futures price is set by the CTD, the bond minimising the basis, defined as *Basis G = P(cash) − CF × F(futures)*. The short futures position is the hedge: if yields rise, the cash bond falls and the short futures gains, and vice versa. The hedge ratio is set by the conversion factor and the DV01 ratio, not by notional alone, a point the quantitative section makes concrete. The CTD is only 111 bp of implied repo ahead of the next bond in the basket (4.5% 11/15/2033 at 2.28% vs 3.39%), so the CTD can switch if yields move, breaking the hedge ratio.

*Repo (the funding leg).* A repurchase agreement is a sale of the bond today plus an agreement to repurchase it shortly after at a slightly higher price; the annualised difference is the repo rate. The lender advances less than the collateral's market value, the _haircut_, so the fund puts up only the haircut in equity and borrows the rest. With a 2% haircut, \$100 of bonds needs \$2 of equity: 50x leverage. Treasury repo haircuts are typically around 3% or less, implying leverage above 30x (Barth & Kahn, OFR Working Paper 21-01); futures initial margin of 1–3% implies 33–99x (Patel, Chicago Fed Letter 516); measured leverage of large basis traders in 2019 averaged 21x (median 17.6x). A zero haircut is possible only when a single intermediary handles both legs. The 50x in our model is the leverage at the assumed 2% haircut, not a claim about typical fund leverage. Only the cash leg needs repo financing; the futures leg needs only margin, but that margin is settled daily in cash, which turns out to be the trade's soft underbelly (Section 3).

These are the securities the course did *not* centre on: the report's mechanism is not a bond's yield in isolation but the interaction of a cash bond, a futures contract on a basket of bonds, and an overnight funding market: three instruments whose risks do not add up the way a single-bond duration calculation suggests.

= Quantitative analysis

*Question.* If the trade is duration-hedged, what can still kill it, and how large is each risk next to the equity that funds it?

*Method.* A representative \$100m-face trade on the actual CTD above, computed from bond math with stated inputs: futures at the official Nov 10 settlement 106.25 (106'080), funding at SOFR 3.88% (effective 9 Nov, NY Fed), haircut 2%, delivery 1 December 2026 (20 days). The cash price is the observed Nov 10 close, 94.188 per 100 face (bid 94.094 / offer 94.125), from Safe Rate Markets' security page republishing Treasury's end-of-day prices; it reprices to a yield of 4.894%, 0.8 bp below CME's reported 4.9023%. Both legs are Nov 10 vintage. (The Nov 11 cash close does not exist: the US bond market was closed on 11 Nov 2026 for Veterans Day.) Our DV01 \$55.03 vs CME's \$53.98 (+1.9%) and our modified duration 5.84 vs CME's 5.78 (+1.0%) cross-check the derivation. The hedge is DV01-matched: *887 contracts*. The base case is computed leg by leg by exact repricing at delivery, not by a duration approximation. Full workings are in Appendix A.

*Result: the trade.* Modified duration 5.84 years (not 8.18: the real CTD has 6.7 years to maturity, not 10), DV01 \$55,034 per bp on the \$100m cash leg, equity \$1.90m, repo borrowing \$93.21m, leverage 50x. Over 20 days to delivery: coupon +\$212,329, repo cost −\$198,173, carry +\$14,156. The basis at entry is *−0.0554 points (cash cheap)*, and it converges to zero at delivery, so the basis P&L is +\$55,425, for a *total of +\$67,850, +3.57% of equity*. That sign is the finding, not a typo: with G = P − CF×F, the clean P&L of the hedged position is exactly dG, so a trade entered with cash _cheap_ earns as the basis converges. The trade earns only when cash is _cheap_ at entry (G < 0). The magnitude is thin and print-dependent: across the Nov 10 prints the total runs +\$67,850 (close, primary) to ~+\$164,000 (bid), with the offer at ~+\$133,000 and the mid at ~+\$148,000, positive throughout (see Appendix A). This corrects the par-bond illustration in Appendix A too: its base case was +\$120,000 with the basis sign flipped; corrected it is −\$80,000.

*Scenario B is rejected.* CME also reports a basis _net of carry_ of −0.3539 points, which implies a cash price near 93.71 and a base-case P&L of +\$368,056 (+19.35% of equity). That cash price is ~38–48c below every Nov 10 print (bid 94.094 / offer 94.125 / close 94.188), so Scenario B is contradicted by the observed close and is not carried as a live scenario; it is kept in Appendix A only for comparison, labelled as rejected. The sign is settled: the base case is positive. What remains is magnitude, and it is print-dependent: across the Nov 10 prints the total runs +\$67,850 (close, primary) to ~+\$164,000 (bid), with the offer at ~+\$133,000 and the mid at ~+\$148,000, positive throughout. The report leads with the close-based +\$67,850 and footnotes the bracket. The close is Treasury's end-of-day print, not a mid (it sits 6.3c above the Nov 10 offer), so the report quotes the close as the close and footnotes the bracket rather than averaging. What does *not* depend on any of this is the stress table below: DV01 and equity move under 2% between the scenarios.

