*Interpretation.* The last two rows are the thesis, and the real CTD makes it sharper than the par illustration did: the duration is shorter (5.84 vs 8.18 years), so the margin call is smaller in dollars (\$2.75m vs \$4.09m), but the equity is smaller too, and the call is still *145% of equity*. A fund with \$1.9m of equity facing a \$2.75m margin call must find cash, sell the bond into a falling market, or default on the margin call. That is a liquidity risk, not a market risk, and no duration number captures it. The same logic applies to the haircut: a lender raising the haircut from 2% to 4% in volatile markets doubles the equity the same position requires, and a fund that does not have it must sell into the same market every other basis fund is selling into. Scaled up, the arithmetic is systemic: at the Fed's \$830bn aggregate, a 0.10-point basis widening is an ~\$830m collective loss, and the gross long-leg DV01 at the real CTD duration is ~\$485m per bp. The market must absorb the selling of the largest, most levered holders in the world's deepest bond market. March 2020 is the precedent for what that absorption looks like when it fails; April 2025 is the warning that the neighbouring swap-spread trade unwinds first and fastest. One more risk the calendar makes concrete: the December 2026 contract expires in weeks, so the whole position must be rolled into March 2027. A roll is itself a moment when funding and margin can bind. The CTD switch is the same risk in miniature: the basket's second bond (4.5% 11/15/2033, implied repo 2.28%) and third (4% 2/15/2034, 1.14%) are close behind the CTD at 3.39%, and a yield move of a few dozen basis points can change which bond is cheapest to deliver. When the CTD switches, the futures contract's DV01 jumps (the third bond's futures DV01 is \$64.54 against the CTD's \$60.85), and a hedge sized for the old CTD is suddenly the wrong size. A fund that does not re-hedge is no longer duration-hedged; a fund that does re-hedge must trade in the same direction as every other basis fund. Finally, the funding assumption deserves a caveat of its own: SOFR is the rate on general-collateral repo, but a hedge fund borrows at a spread over it, and that spread widens exactly when the fund most needs to borrow. Our repo shock (+50 bp costs only \$25,538) looks small only because 20 days remain to delivery; the same shock on a 3-month horizon would cost roughly \$116,000, and on a position rolled quarter after quarter it compounds.

A second leverage channel sits in the futures leg itself: initial-margin leverage in Treasury futures was about 175x (5-year) and 120x (10-year) before the pandemic, and about 70x and 50x since 2021 as volatility raised margin requirements (BIS Quarterly Review, September 2023, Box A). A rise in initial margin mechanically forces deleveraging (post cash or close positions), and disorderly margin deleveraging exacerbated distress in both September 2019 and March 2020.

*Limitations.* The base-case P&L is print-dependent (see the magnitude bracket above); the funding rate is SOFR, a proxy for a hedge fund's actual repo rate, which is higher; futures initial margin is treated as a liquidity demand, not modelled as a return drag; and the aggregate scaling assumes the representative duration applies to the whole \$830bn book, which it does not exactly. None of these changes the ranking of the risks: funding and margin dominate market risk in every sensitivity we ran. The close is Treasury's end-of-day print, not a mid (it sits 6.3c above the Nov 10 offer), so the report quotes the close as the close and footnotes the bracket rather than averaging.

= Implications

*For intermediaries.* Dealers and repo lenders are the trade's plumbing and its first line of defence. When volatility rises they raise haircuts and margin, rationally, fund by fund, and collectively create the fire sale they are protecting themselves against. Prime brokers face the counterparty channel: the failure of one large basis fund imposes losses on its lenders and forces sales of the collateral they seize.

*For investors.* Asset managers are on the other side of the futures: their long futures demand is, in the Chicago Fed's telling, what the basis trade translates into cash-market liquidity. That is the trade's genuine social value: it makes Treasuries more liquid in normal times. The cost is that the liquidity is provided by the most fragile holders, and it can vanish in stress, exactly when it is needed. Hedge-fund investors face the same asymmetry as the funds: a strategy that earns a few basis points, levered 50x, and can lose multiples of its equity in a margin call.

*For the public.* A Treasury-market seizure is not an abstract market event: Treasury yields set mortgage rates, corporate borrowing costs and the discount rate for everything else. In March 2020 only large-scale Fed purchases stopped a bond-market rout from becoming a broader financial crisis. The public pays for that insurance, in the form of central-bank intervention, while the profits in calm times are private.

*For regulators.* The reform agenda follows directly from the mechanism: mandatory central clearing of Treasury transactions (SEC, Release No. 34-99149, December 2023): cash transactions by 31 December 2026 and repo by 30 June 2027 (extended one year, February 2025). But with a scope limit that matters: the repo leg that funds the trade is in scope, while a hedge fund's cash purchase from a dealer is not, so clearing reaches the funding and margin channel, not the cash leg. Central clearing replaces bilateral exposures with a clearinghouse and standardised margin; the FSB's final report (July 2025, Recommendations 4–5) backs the same menu (minimum haircuts in government-bond repos, enhanced margin, central clearing), so leverage cannot silently build to 100x in calm times; better data: the Fed's decomposition itself exists because Form PF does not report trade-level positions, so the \$830bn is an estimate, not a measurement; and the contested idea of a basis purchase facility, proposed not by the Fed but by Kashyap, Stein, Wallen & Younger (Brookings Papers on Economic Activity, Spring 2025): hedged purchases in which the Fed buys cash Treasuries and shorts the equivalent futures, duration-neutral, either as a standing cap on the basis or as auctions of bundled basis packages, with penalty pricing to limit moral hazard, which would socialise the unwind risk the trade creates. Each remedy trades off the liquidity the trade provides against the fragility it creates, and the report's view is that clearing and margin floors address the mechanism, while a purchase facility addresses the symptom.

= Team takeaways

Three things we will remember. First, a hedge is not a hedge against everything: this trade is hedged against the risk it looks exposed to (yields) and exposed to the risks it does not look exposed to (funding, haircuts, margin timing). Second, leverage is not a footnote to a small edge: at 50–100x, the funding terms *are* the trade, and a 2-percentage-point haircut change is a 100% capital call. Third, individually rational trades can be collectively catastrophic: each fund's decision to sell when its margin is called is sensible, and all of them selling at once is a crisis. The fixed-income lesson is that price risk and liquidity risk are different risks, and the Treasury market, the world's "risk-free" market, is where that difference matters most.

