= Appendix C: AI critique (~0.5 page) [RUN 2026-11-16]

Prompt used (verbatim): "Explain the Treasury cash-futures basis trade and why it is considered low-risk: a hedge fund buys cash Treasuries, sells Treasury futures, finances the bonds in repo, and earns the basis as it converges to zero at delivery. What are the risks?"

The prompt was run on 16 Nov 2026 on a fresh AI assistant given only the prompt (no report, no data; full response saved in `data/ai-response-2026-11-16.md`).

Summary of the AI response: The response explains the trade as a duration-hedged relative-value position that earns the basis as it converges to zero at delivery, funded in repo, and correctly identifies the risks as funding and liquidity rather than interest rates: repo spikes, daily variation margin that can exceed equity, haircut increases, basis widening, CTD switches, rollover, and crowded-trade correlation. It never calls the trade risk-free and invents no figures.

Critique (checked against the actual response; the draft's predicted errors did not all survive the check): Two of the draft's expected errors are not in this response and are dropped, not asserted: the response does not list interest-rate risk as the main risk (it says the risks are "mostly about funding and liquidity rather than interest rates"), and it does cover the cheapest-to-deliver switch. It also makes no aggregate claim, so there is no \$830bn error to correct. What it does get wrong: (1) Leverage as typical. The response says haircuts are "often around 2%" and "50x is common". Verified sources say haircuts are typically around 3% or less, and measured leverage of large basis traders in 2019 averaged 21x (median 17.6x) (Barth & Kahn, OFR Working Paper 21-01). 50x is the leverage at the assumed 2% haircut in our model, not a typical figure. The response presents a model assumption as a market fact. (2) The sign condition on convergence. The response says the fund "earns the basis as it converges to zero" and "locks in the basis it bought at entry", without saying convergence only earns when cash is cheap at entry. With Basis G = P − CF×F, the clean P&L of the hedged position is exactly dG: entered with cash cheap (G < 0) it earns, entered with cash rich (G > 0) it loses. Our model: the Nov 10 entry had G = −0.0554 (cash cheap) and convergence earned +\$55,425; the par illustration's original +\$120,000 base case had this sign flipped and corrected to −\$80,000. The response's phrasing would lead a reader to think convergence always pays. What the response gets right matters too: it states the margin call can exceed equity (in our model 144.7% of equity on a 50 bp yield fall), names the haircut channel, the March 2020 widening, and the crowded-trade correlation, the mechanism this report is built on. Correction: the trade is duration-hedged and funding-exposed; its risk is liquidity, leverage and correlation-to-one failure in stress, as the March 2020 precedent shows.

